      <h1>Your 2026 Benefits Expire December 31: <em>Use Them Before They're Gone</em></h1>
      <p>Most Canadian extended health plans reset on January 1, and unused maximums usually don't roll over. Here is how to check what you have left, and how to use it well.</p>
      <p class="byline">By WellFinder · November 2026 · 7 min read</p>
    </div>
      </div>
    
  </section>

  <section class="section--tight">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="article-wrap">
        <div class="article-body">

          <p>It is November, and there is a good chance you are sitting on benefits you have already paid for.</p>
          <p>Most Canadian extended health plans run on the calendar year. For plans like these, the annual maximums for services like massage therapy, physiotherapy, naturopathic care, and psychology reset on January 1 — and the unused portion usually does not roll over.</p>
          <p>That massage coverage you meant to use in the spring? The physiotherapy amount still showing in your app? If your plan works this way, whatever is left on December 31 is simply gone.</p>
          <p>Not every plan works this way, and we will get to the exceptions below. But the first step is the same for everyone: check what you actually have left, before the year runs out.</p>

          <h2>How to check what you have left</h2>
          <p>Start with your insurer's app or member portal. Most show a running balance for each type of service, so you can see how much of your annual maximum remains for physiotherapy, massage, naturopathy, psychology, and other paramedical services.</p>
          <p>If the app is unclear, your plan booklet is the next stop. It is the document your employer or insurer gave you when you enrolled, and it spells out exactly what your plan covers. The CLHIA's consumer guide to supplementary health insurance makes the same point: it is very important to know what deductibles, coinsurance percentages, and maximums apply to you, and to check your plan literature carefully.</p>
          <p>As you look, watch for two different kinds of limits:</p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>A per-service annual maximum</strong> — for example, a total dollar amount for physiotherapy for the year.</li>
            <li><strong>A per-visit limit</strong> — a cap on how much the plan will pay toward any single appointment, even if your annual maximum is not used up.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>Your plan may also pay only a percentage of each visit, called coinsurance, and some plans have a small deductible to meet at the start of the plan year.</p>
          <p>One detail that matters at year-end: claims are based on the date you actually receive the service, not the date you book it or pay for it. An appointment on January 3 counts against next year's maximum, even if you booked and paid in December.</p>
          <p>If you cannot find your balance in the app or booklet, your employer's HR or benefits team can tell you where to look, or confirm the details with the insurer for you.</p>

          <h2>What books up first in November and December</h2>
          <p>You are not the only one doing this math in November.</p>
          <p>Paramedical appointments fill up quickly at this time of year, as everyone with remaining benefits tries to fit visits in before the reset. Massage therapists, physiotherapists, and psychologists in particular can be booked out weeks ahead by late November.</p>
          <p>Some services need even more lead time. A psychoeducational assessment, for example, can involve a waitlist, an intake, and several appointments spread over weeks. If you have been meaning to book one for yourself or your child, November is the time to get on the list and start the process — not mid-December.</p>
          <p>The practical move is simple: book now, even for appointments a few weeks out. A December appointment booked in early November is a plan. The same appointment requested on December 20 is often a waitlist spot.</p>

          <h2>Smart ways people use remaining benefits</h2>
          <p>Leftover benefits do not have to mean a last-minute scramble for whatever appointment is open. This is also a genuinely good time to start something you have been curious about — the assessment or first session now, while this year's coverage can help, and a clearer picture of whether you want to continue in the new year.</p>
          <p>Here are some of the options people find through WellFinder, and how they are typically billed. Coverage is never automatic: it depends on who provides the service, how it is billed, and the details of your plan.</p>
          <p><strong>Start a clinical Pilates program.</strong> Clinical Pilates begins with an assessment by a physiotherapist, who then builds a program around your body and your goals. When a registered physiotherapist provides it as physiotherapy, sessions may be eligible under your physiotherapy coverage.</p>
          <p><strong>Get a TPI golf assessment in the off-season.</strong> A TPI assessment looks at how your body moves through your golf swing, and where mobility or strength is holding you back. It is delivered by a regulated health professional such as a physiotherapist and billed under that discipline. Winter is when many golfers do this work, so spring arrives with a plan instead of a sore back.</p>
          <p><strong>Try microneedling or cosmetic acupuncture through a naturopath.</strong> When microneedling or cosmetic acupuncture is provided by a registered naturopathic doctor as part of naturopathic care, it may be billed under your naturopathic coverage. The same treatment at a spa, provided by someone without a regulated credential your plan recognizes, generally cannot be claimed the same way — who provides it is the whole question.</p>
          <p><strong>Start couples therapy.</strong> Couples therapy is provided by psychologists, registered social workers, and other regulated therapists, and is billed under the discipline of the person providing it. If you and your partner each have coverage, ask the clinic how receipts are issued, since that can affect how you submit your claims.</p>
          <p><strong>Look into neurofeedback.</strong> Neurofeedback is often offered through psychology practices and billed as psychological services when provided or supervised by a registered psychologist. If psychology is one of your larger unused maximums, it may be worth asking a clinic how their sessions are billed.</p>
          <p><strong>Finally see someone about pelvic floor physiotherapy.</strong> Pelvic floor physiotherapy is physiotherapy, provided by a physiotherapist with additional training in pelvic health. It is one of the most commonly put-off appointments — and one of the most commonly covered, if your plan includes physiotherapy.</p>
          <p><strong>Ask about IV or IM vitamin therapy.</strong> Vitamin infusions and injections are offered by naturopathic doctors and billed under naturopathic coverage when an ND provides them. An assessment is usually the first step, which makes November a sensible time to have that first conversation.</p>
          <p>None of these are guaranteed to be covered under your specific plan. Think of them as well-informed places to start asking questions — which brings us to the questions.</p>

          <h2>Before you book: five questions</h2>
          <p>A few minutes of asking can save you a declined claim in January. Before you book, ask the clinic — and, where noted, your insurer:</p>
          <ol>
            <li><strong>Who will provide my session?</strong> Coverage usually follows the regulated professional, not the name of the treatment.</li>
            <li><strong>What will the service be billed as?</strong> Physiotherapy, naturopathic care, psychology, and social work are separate buckets in your plan, each with its own maximum.</li>
            <li><strong>What will my receipt show?</strong> Your insurer will want the provider's name, their credential and registration details, the service, and the date. Ask before you are standing at the front desk.</li>
            <li><strong>Do you offer direct billing?</strong> If the clinic bills your insurer directly, you may only pay any difference at the visit. If not, you pay up front and submit the receipt yourself. Either can work — just know which one you are getting.</li>
            <li><strong>What is my remaining balance?</strong> This one is for your insurer's app or your plan booklet. Check it against both your annual maximum and any per-visit limit, so a December surprise does not become a January bill.</li>
          </ol>
          <p>If anything is still unclear, a quick call to your insurer before the appointment is worth it. For larger expenses, some plans will even estimate in advance how much they would pay — the CLHIA calls this a predetermination of benefits.</p>

          <h2>What if your plan carries forward?</h2>
          <p>Some plans are more forgiving, so do not assume the December 31 deadline applies to you without checking.</p>
          <p>Health spending accounts are the most common exception. Under Canada Revenue Agency guidance for private health services plans, a health care spending account is allowed to carry forward either your unused balance or your unpaid eligible expenses — but not both — for up to 12 months, without losing its status as a private health services plan. Many employers use exactly that design. Others choose no carry-forward at all.</p>
          <p>Some group plans also run on a plan year that is not the calendar year, or build in a grace period for submitting claims after year-end. Those details live in your plan booklet, not in a general rule.</p>
          <p>So check before you assume either way. If your plan does carry something forward, you can plan calmly instead of rushing. If it does not, you still have time — if you start now.</p>

          <h2>A calm way to finish the year</h2>
          <p>You do not need to use every dollar, and booking appointments you do not need helps no one. But if there is a sore shoulder you have been ignoring, an assessment you keep postponing, or a treatment you have been meaning to ask about, your remaining 2026 benefits may be the nudge — and the budget — to finally start.</p>
          <p>Check your balance this week. Book the assessment while there is still room in the calendar. And when January comes, you will start the new plan year having actually used the one you paid for.</p>
          <p>You can search WellFinder's directory by treatment and by city to find clinics across Canada — including who provides each treatment and, where clinics offer it, whether direct billing is available. Start with the treatment you have been curious about, and ask the five questions above before you book.</p>

          <h2>Sources and further reading</h2>
          <ul>
            <li><a href="http://clhia.uberflip.com/i/199452-a-guide-to-supplementary-health-insurance/6" target="_blank" rel="noopener">A Guide to Supplementary Health Insurance — CLHIA</a> — The Canadian Life and Health Insurance Association's consumer guide explains deductibles, coinsurance, and maximums in plain language, and why your plan booklet is the document that matters.</li>
